1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,617
Ninety five dollars.
That is every paid tool in this build, added up.

2
00:00:04,867 --> 00:00:10,659
Everything else is free or open source.
And one file in there was never supposed to ship at all.

3
00:00:14,509 --> 00:00:22,861
PengPong is a survivors-like where a penguin with a hockey stick whacks whatever is in front of it.
Guns. Pufferfish. Other penguins.

4
00:00:23,111 --> 00:00:28,668
It's from a Korean studio called SANDY FLOOR,
and the full game came out on August twentieth.

5
00:00:28,918 --> 00:00:35,546
The demo sits at eighty six percent, the full game at eighty five.
Nearly all of the praise is for the art.

6
00:00:35,796 --> 00:00:43,809
Nineteen thirties rubber-hose cartoon. Cuphead comes up in review after review.
That look normally costs a lot of money.

7
00:00:44,059 --> 00:00:48,780
So that was my question.
What did this team buy, and what did they build.

8
00:00:49,030 --> 00:00:57,173
Steam publishes the list of files a demo installs.
PengPong's is two hundred and eighty two files, two point six eight gigabytes.

9
00:00:57,423 --> 00:01:01,204
The first clue is a folder name.
MonoBleedingEdge.

10
00:01:01,454 --> 00:01:12,523
Unity builds your code one of two ways.
IL2CPP turns the C# into native code and fuses it into one block. Mono leaves the DLLs as they are.

11
00:01:12,773 --> 00:01:17,991
PengPong is Mono.
So everything this team pulled in is sitting there under its own file name.

12
00:01:18,241 --> 00:01:23,276
Audio is FMOD. Four sound bank files,
twenty nine megabytes.

13
00:01:23,526 --> 00:01:32,244
Take this one with you. FMOD is free below two hundred thousand dollars a year in revenue.
There is no reason an indie can't use commercial audio middleware.

14
00:01:32,494 --> 00:01:38,966
The paid items are exactly two. Text Animator,
sixty five dollars. Ara Trails, thirty.

15
00:01:39,216 --> 00:01:45,322
DOTween, GameAnalytics, UIParticle,
NavMeshPlus. All free, or open source.

16
00:01:45,572 --> 00:01:53,924
So what did they animate that Cuphead look with. Normally that's Spine,
and a personal licence for Spine is three hundred and forty nine dollars.

17
00:01:54,174 --> 00:02:01,220
There is no Spine in this build.
There is Unity's own 2D Animation package, and the IK package next to it.

18
00:02:01,470 --> 00:02:07,863
Same idea. You put bones in a drawing and move the bones.
And you get it from the Package Manager for nothing.

19
00:02:08,113 --> 00:02:13,905
Now the strangest thing in the list.
Assembly-CSharp.dll is five kilobytes.

20
00:02:14,155 --> 00:02:22,638
Unless you tell it otherwise, Unity puts all of your game code in that one file.
Five kilobytes means the code has been moved out.

21
00:02:22,888 --> 00:02:32,938
The mechanism is called an Assembly Definition, asmdef for short.
One small file in a folder, and that folder compiles to its own DLL.

22
00:02:33,188 --> 00:02:42,638
What you get is compile time. Change one line and only that module rebuilds,
not the whole project. The bigger the project, the bigger the difference.

23
00:02:42,888 --> 00:02:53,644
Eight modules came out of this, and every one carries the studio's name.
SandyToolkit. SandyAudio. SandyPooling. SandySetting. SandyDependency.

24
00:02:53,894 --> 00:03:02,508
Pooling, audio, settings, dependency injection. The things you rewrite for every game.
This team carries them to the next project as folders.

25
00:03:02,758 --> 00:03:09,752
Saves go through SQLite. With over three hundred items,
that beats one big JSON file.

26
00:03:10,002 --> 00:03:18,616
Content is split into seventeen Addressables bundles,
five hundred and twenty nine megabytes. A patch only downloads the bundles that changed.

27
00:03:18,866 --> 00:03:22,987
There's a Localization package too.
Eight languages go through it.

28
00:03:23,237 --> 00:03:32,843
And then two things that should not be in a release build. The first is Unity Recorder,
an editor tool for capturing video. Harmless. Somebody forgot to take it out.

29
00:03:33,093 --> 00:03:40,453
The second is MCP for Unity.
That's the open source bridge that lets an AI model drive the Unity editor directly.

30
00:03:40,703 --> 00:03:47,384
What I can confirm is that the file is in the shipped build.
How much they used it, and for what, only the developer knows.

31
00:03:47,634 --> 00:03:54,811
My guess is it was wired up for development and went out with everything else.
If I'm wrong, say so in the comments and I'll pin the correction.

32
00:03:55,061 --> 00:04:05,347
For scale: of the two point six eight gigabytes, art and sound are two point two.
All one hundred and ninety five DLLs together are ninety one megabytes.

33
00:04:05,597 --> 00:04:11,755
Now the reviews of the full game,
where the complaints land in one place. It launched with three stages.

34
00:04:12,005 --> 00:04:18,764
Tools outlive the game they were built for.
But the hours that went into them are hours this game's content did not get.

35
00:04:19,014 --> 00:04:23,788
Eight frameworks and three stages.
Those two numbers come from the same team.

36
00:04:24,038 --> 00:04:26,435
Here's the whole teardown on one card.

37
00:04:26,685 --> 00:04:36,971
Unity, Mono. Ninety five dollars of paid assets.
Unity 2D Animation instead of Spine. Eight in-house modules. Three stages.

38
00:04:37,221 --> 00:04:42,360
Worth a dip. The demo is free,
and hitting things with that stick feels as good as it looks.

39
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:54,149
Three things to take away. FMOD is free for indies. You can do bone animation without paying for Spine.
And splitting your code into asmdefs is what makes a big project compile fast.

40
00:04:54,399 --> 00:05:04,789
That's my read from the outside. If you made this and I got something wrong,
say so and I'll pin the correction. Next time, another demo, another file list. This is Demo Dip.
